## What is this?
Kolori ("to color" in Esperanto) is an application that allows you to create
beautiful images from mathematical functions. 

### Gallery
See the `gallery/` directory for a gallery of generated images.

## How to use?

### Controls
- Use your mouse cursor/WASD/arrow keys to pan. If you are on a touchscreen
device you may also simply use your finger.
- Scroll or "+"/"-" to zoom in/out.
- `h` to hide the "Settings" window.
- `r` to reload shaders.
- `c` to toggle cursor visibility.
- `p` to pause animations. Animations will unpause when switching functions.
- `ESC` to reset zoom and pan.
- `F11` to toggle fullscreen.
- `F12` to take a screenshot. It will be saved in the curent woking directory
with filename `kolori_screenshotDD-MM-YYYY_HHMMSS.png`.

### Function input
Input a valid mathematical expression into the "Function" input box using the
variable "z". Only the "+", "-", "*", "/", and "^" operators are supported 
currently. We plan to implement the "%" operator in the future. Implicit
multiplication is also allowed. A list of supported functions is found at
the top of `translator.odin`. 

For animations, you may also use the scalar variable "t" (stands for "time").
- Example: "z * e^(t*i)" to rotate the plane.

You may control how fast "t" increases using the "Speed" slider in the
"Settings" window. The speed has units of "units per second"--a speed of 1
means that "t" will inrease by 1 every second. While the slider is clampted
to 0.1-10, you may input arbitrary values if you ctrl+click on the slider.

## How to build?

### Prerequisites
This project is made in the Odin programming language. As such, you will need
to install the Odin compiler to build. Instructions for installation are held
here: <https://odin-lang.org/docs/install/>

We also use Python to build the ImGui binaries. If you haven't already,
install Python from here: <https://www.python.org/downloads/>

Finally, on Linux/Mac OS X, install SDL3 using your system's package manager
(`apt`, `pacman`, `brew`, etc.).

### Steps
The first three steps you will only need to perform once. After that, simply 
execute

```sh
$ odin build .
```

to build the entire project.

1. Clone the git repository and its submodules:

```sh
$ git clone --recursive https://github.com/neroist/kolori.git
$ cd kolori
```

2. Modify the Python build script at `odin-imgui/build.py` as such:

```diff
- wanted_backends = ["vulkan", "sdl2", "sdl3", "sdlrenderer3", "sdlgpu3", "opengl3", "glfw",
-				     "dx11", "dx12", "osx", "metal", "null", "wgpu", "webgl"]
+ wanted_backends = ["sdl3", "opengl3"]
```

You may also change the default compiler from `cl` (on Windows) or 
`clang` (everywhere else) to another compiler if need be. We leave
such modifications to you. However, in the case that you need to 
compile with `gcc` instead of `clang`, it is sufficient to do a
simple search-and-replace operation on the entire file.

3. Run the build script

```sh
$ cd odin-imgui
$ python build.py
```

4. Build the project

```sh
$ cd ..
$ odin build src -out:kolori
```
